#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548103} :activity/id #uuid "8f3ab43f-9a2a-52cf-8072-18ee6e695302" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048694 :topic/id #uuid "b52b86c6-5073-5d60-a17c-32a308489156" :topic/math-academy-id 2638} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548103 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345237 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12b1571f-f5f7-562d-80a0-607aa3516530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342403} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9c1e54-997f-58a0-bd84-3b903059ab31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (3-{cos}^{6}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342870} {:answer/id #uuid "7da69662-1fe5-58e3-b0c0-5897eb5c94d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343322} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd26a22-60c5-5518-ab97-c388fe07fef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344065} {:answer/id #uuid "d1097377-be4e-5b0d-8118-afb24f1f0f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344076}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343322} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d5e348e-6403-58e5-a33c-30c8b85d2e1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d123bbf4-7b8c-5b08-b206-9ffde691a39b" :question/math-academy-id "q-76610" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}-2{({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,(1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,(1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4fb0570e-604f-5e03-b57b-2ef557a8e16c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548133}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455435 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cb0f1f8-2c8c-587d-af87-78c647b1cad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454969} {:answer/id #uuid "52950150-cf38-5785-b5c4-0a95b436b499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2({e}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455050} {:answer/id #uuid "84793b57-cdb0-527c-8f47-4fc489aedbaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e({e}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455140} {:answer/id #uuid "928594cf-1e82-52f9-95c7-79551dbc7ad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e({e}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455169} {:answer/id #uuid "adebc696-6cad-5a00-9edf-015f1843d18f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2({e}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455208}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455140} :answer-field/id #uuid "61a3ae46-0e2c-5017-bfca-74625ceef8e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1b9e5c87-171d-5626-ae59-fa7b69d9a95a" :question/math-academy-id "q-76766" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y+1}$ along the polar curve $r=2$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y+1}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{e}^{y+1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,$ $\\text{d}u=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{e}^{u}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3}-2e \\\\  & =2e({e}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6b56d38e-560a-5855-970e-c59d2f999047" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548127}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548127 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3978ab12-c2c3-556b-8b3e-3e2439bb98af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362991} {:answer/id #uuid "921abcc9-664f-5748-a32a-b8cb3a3a1679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362992} {:answer/id #uuid "55926885-53a5-5ed5-b872-7944d1c6177d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362993} {:answer/id #uuid "87181d2e-ae8e-5568-bad0-b0d06ff451c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362994} {:answer/id #uuid "70de9d14-e9f3-5545-9a7f-d37446be4ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362995}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362991} :answer-field/id #uuid "c54a12c4-ac53-57bf-ac59-6e5331dff070" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "05fbe250-8eb4-5b81-83cc-08a815c1aec0" :question/math-academy-id "q-123510" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ along the polar curve $r=2$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =8{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}8{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}{u}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}({1}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "85c711d0-5b3c-52f0-81be-22f51de6f340"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548133 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "404520d3-d967-5b0b-9a92-89f881bf696e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455012} {:answer/id #uuid "890fe5ec-8858-5534-8b26-e75adee4e582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455150} {:answer/id #uuid "90adc149-a9e2-5b9c-8313-fbc0425f524a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455166} {:answer/id #uuid "e503b46f-e7bf-57d7-8559-2e0601cdad27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455298} {:answer/id #uuid "ef25c8f6-c622-5edf-b418-bcdc116be329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455318}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455012} :answer-field/id #uuid "63750d47-51f3-5cdf-a76b-72522571295c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3f1c8267-786a-5d55-8970-d1a062c41c69" :question/math-academy-id "q-76673" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}$ along the polar curve $r=1$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8f5cb91b-d739-5769-854c-6dbdff5a72c8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548160}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548160 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7eee1f5-4e67-5791-ab19-b18c7b3e839f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343993} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc4b0b3-3161-5573-8ff6-5f7ebe4c7119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344166} {:answer/id #uuid "e210fd40-0385-5c02-968e-6174892fbc40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344228} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c07b9f-3789-507f-97de-8e306b0fb0a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344384} {:answer/id #uuid "f5abf403-18d1-5fd6-8efd-732e33e8d35e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344400}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344384} :answer-field/id #uuid "7df0e8e5-c868-5ece-818b-a9164009ba5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6d3824d0-05f9-53c5-8fe3-dac14910620a" :question/math-academy-id "q-76694" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}$ along the polar curve $r=2$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{3}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}4\\sqrt{2}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{u}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}({(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5/2}-{0}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{5}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c76ec6eb-64e7-5508-82da-b33da64af211" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548117}}] :activity/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Polar Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547303}]}
